<article class="env-lemma" id="0B02">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B02">Lemma <span data-tag="0B02">43.16.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a nonsingular variety and $W, V \subset X$ closed subvarieties which intersect properly. Let $Z$ be an irreducible component of $V \cap W$ with generic point $\xi $. Assume that $\mathcal{O}_{W, \xi }$ and $\mathcal{O}_{V, \xi }$ are Cohen-Macaulay. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  e(X, V \cdot W, Z) = \text{length}_{\mathcal{O}_{X, \xi }}(\mathcal{O}_{V \cap W, \xi })  \]
</div>
  <p> where $V \cap W$ is the scheme theoretic intersection. In particular, if both $V$ and $W$ are Cohen-Macaulay, then $V \cdot W = [V \cap W]_{\dim (V) + \dim (W) - \dim (X)}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $A = \mathcal{O}_{X, \xi }$, $B = \mathcal{O}_{V, \xi }$, and $C = \mathcal{O}_{W, \xi }$. By Auslander-Buchsbaum (Algebra, Proposition <a href="/tag/090V" data-tag="090V">10.111.1</a>) we can find a finite free resolution $F_\bullet \to B$ of length 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{depth}(A) - \text{depth}(B) = \dim (A) - \dim (B) = \dim (C)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       First equality as $A$ and $B$ are Cohen-Macaulay and the second as $V$ and $W$ intersect properly. Then $F_\bullet \otimes _ A C$ is a complex of finite free modules representing $B \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} C$ hence has cohomology modules with support in $\{ \mathfrak m_ A\} $. By the Acyclicity lemma (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00N0" data-tag="00N0">10.102.8</a>) which applies as $C$ is Cohen-Macaulay we conclude that $F_\bullet \otimes _ A C$ has nonzero cohomology only in degree $0$. This finishes the proof. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>